.he "rlogind (8)                     11/03/26                   rlogind (8)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
rlogind
- the remote login server
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
/bin/rlogind [ port ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Rlogind listens on a tcp socket and serves the rlogin request over the
network card (sys/NET.md).  The port defaults to 513; the command-line
port exists for the simulator, which binds a high host port for the
guest.
.sp
Each connection is one request:
.sp
.nf
.in +4
<empty> NUL <luser> NUL <ruser> NUL <term>/<speed> NUL
.in -4
.fi
.sp
The first field is the client's separate terminal type when it sends
one and is empty otherwise; the terminal type and speed ride in the
fourth as <term>/<speed>, with any further arguments after more
slashes.  This daemon reads the two user names and ignores the terminal.
.sp
The caller is checked against /etc/hosts.equiv and the remote user's
~/.rhosts (/.rhosts for an account whose home is /), by address: the
card's connected event carries the peer's dotted quad.  Root is never
trusted through hosts.equiv, only through its own .rhosts.  There is no
password and no encryption, so an untrusted caller is refused rather
than asked to log in.
.sp
A trusted request is answered with one NUL byte, as RFC 1282 does, or
with a byte of value 1 and a message when it is refused.  Everything
after that byte is the terminal.  The daemon puts a login shell on a
pty slave and shuttles bytes between the master and the connection,
which gives the shell its terminal, its line discipline and its job
control.  There are two pairs, /dev/ptyp0 with /dev/ttyp0 and
/dev/ptyp1 with /dev/ttyp1; a session claims one by trying each and the
driver refuses a pair another session holds.  If both pairs are held
the connection is closed.
.sp
There is no command form.  Running a command is rshd (8)'s business:
rsh (1) with a command speaks the rsh request, and with no command it
execs rlogin, which is this protocol.  The window-size negotiation the
classic daemon offers is not done here.
.sp
The daemon forks a child per request.  The parent keeps the listener:
LISTEN returns when the card has a connection parked - its one pending
slot - and ACCEPT hands the child the connected socket while the parent
goes back to listening, so two sessions run at once.  A finished child
sends the parent SIGTERM and is reaped on the next pass; a one-second
alarm bounds the wait for a card with no interrupt line.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/dev/net0	the card's socket clone
.br
/dev/ptyp0, /dev/ttyp0	the first pty pair
.br
/dev/ptyp1, /dev/ttyp1	the second
.br
/etc/hosts.equiv	hosts trusted for ordinary users
.br
~/.rhosts	hosts and users trusted for this account
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
rlogin (1), rshd (8), rsh (1), sys/NET.md
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
The authentication is by address, and the request carries the user
names in clear.  The terminal type and window size are ignored, so the
guest's idea of the screen is whatever its pty driver defaults to.
